      "heading": "Side Effects, Precautions, and Warnings for Blood Sugar",
      "body": "<p>Common side effects reported in clinical studies include mild gastrointestinal upset, such as diarrhea or bloating, especially when starting at higher doses. These symptoms typically resolve within the first two weeks as the body adapts. The mechanism behind the GI disturbances is thought to involve the modulation of gut motility and fluid secretion by the active phytochemicals in CelluFend, which can transiently increase intestinal transit time. Patients with type 2 diabetes should monitor for any signs of hypoglycemia, particularly if they are on insulin or sulfonylureas. The risk of hypoglycemia is modest because CelluFend primarily improves insulin sensitivity rather than directly stimulating insulin release; however, when combined with agents that increase insulin secretion, the additive effect can lower blood glucose levels more than anticipated.</p>\n<p><strong>Drug Interaction Table</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table class=\"md-table\"><thead><tr><th class=\"tbl-th\">Medication</th><th class=\"tbl-th\">Interaction Risk</th><th class=\"tbl-th\">Recommendation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=\"tbl-row-even\"><td class=\"tbl-td\">Insulin</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">May increase hypoglycemia risk</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">Consult your prescribing doctor before combining</td></tr><tr class=\"tbl-row-odd\"><td class=\"tbl-td\">Metformin</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">No significant interaction</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">Continue as directed</td></tr><tr class=\"tbl-row-even\"><td class=\"tbl-td\">ACE inhibitors</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">No significant interaction</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">Continue as directed</td></tr><tr class=\"tbl-row-odd\"><td class=\"tbl-td\">Statins</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">No significant interaction</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">Continue as directed</td></tr><tr class=\"tbl-row-even\"><td class=\"tbl-td\">GLP‑1 agonists</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">No significant interaction</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">Continue as directed</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"tbl-source\">Source: Farrag et al., 2022</p>\n<p><strong>Warning</strong>: If you experience persistent nausea, severe diarrhea, or signs of hypoglycemia, discontinue use and seek medical advice. Always review all current medications with your healthcare provider before starting CelluFend. Action tip: Review all current medications with your doctor before starting.</p>\n<p>In addition to the gastrointestinal profile, rare reports of mild skin irritation at the application site have been noted, likely due to the excipient base. No serious adverse events such as liver toxicity or renal impairment have been documented in the 12‑month trial period. Nonetheless, patients with pre‑existing hepatic or renal disease should have baseline function tests and periodic monitoring, as the bioactive compounds are partially metabolized by the liver and excreted renally.</p>\n<p>Patients should also be aware that while CelluFend does not directly lower blood glucose, its antioxidant and anti‑inflammatory properties may enhance the efficacy of conventional antidiabetic drugs. This synergistic effect can lead to a more pronounced reduction in HbA1c, but it also necessitates careful titration of insulin or sulfonylureas to avoid inadvertent hypoglycemia. Regular self‑monitoring of capillary glucose, especially during the first month of therapy, is therefore recommended.</p>\n<p>Finally, adherence to a balanced diet and regular physical activity remains essential. CelluFend should be viewed as an adjunct to, not a replacement for, established lifestyle modifications and pharmacotherapy. By integrating these measures, patients can maximize glycemic control while minimizing the risk of adverse events.</p>\n",
